<section class="how-it-works section-padded" id="how-it-works">
    <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-eyebrow">Quick Setup</div>
        <h2>Up and running in minutes.</h2>
        <p>Three steps and Halo is ready to go on your Mac.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
        <li class="step">
            <span class="step-number">1</span>
            <div class="step-content">
                <h3>Download &amp; Install</h3>
                <p>Click the Download button, open the <code>.dmg</code>, and drag Halo to Applications. No Homebrew, no Node, no Python — just a native Mac app.</p>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
            <span class="step-number">2</span>
            <div class="step-content">
                <h3>Grant Permissions</h3>
                <p>On first launch, Halo asks for Microphone and Accessibility access. You can also optionally grant Full Disk Access for reading protected folders. Settings has one-tap shortcuts to the relevant macOS permission panes.</p>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
            <span class="step-number">3</span>
            <div class="step-content">
                <h3>Say "Mercy" &amp; Go</h3>
                <p>Halo lives in your menu bar. Click the icon or say <strong>"Mercy"</strong> to open the Liquid Glass panel. Ask anything — it types, taps, searches, and replies in your own Mac voice.</p>
            </div>
        </li>
    </ol>
    <div class="how-note">
        <h4>Can't open it? macOS Gatekeeper</h4>
        <p>Because Halo is not yet distributed through the Mac App Store, macOS may show a security warning on first launch. Right-click (or Control-click) <code>Halo.app</code> and choose <strong>Open</strong>, then confirm. You only need to do this once.</p>
    </div>
</section>

